VT_TALK("sql/sql-joins", {
 "inner": "A join puts rows from two tables side by side. An order stores only a customer number, so to show the customer's name we pair each order with the customer whose id matches. The pairing rule goes after the word ON. An inner join, which is what plain JOIN means, keeps only the pairs where the rule is true, so customers who never ordered simply disappear from the result. Watch the animation pair each customer with their orders. Short aliases like o and c save typing, and when both tables have a column called id, you must say which one you mean. Think of matching left shoes with right shoes, only complete pairs go into the box.",
 "outer": "An inner join drops rows with no partner, but often you want them. LEFT JOIN keeps every row of the left table, and fills the right side with NULL when there is no match. That is how you list every customer with their order count, including zero. Use COUNT of the order id, not COUNT star, because COUNT star would count the padded row as one. RIGHT JOIN keeps every row of the right table, and FULL JOIN keeps everything from both sides. A neat trick called the anti join keeps only the NULL padded rows, which finds customers who never ordered. SQLite added RIGHT and FULL joins in 2022, while MySQL still lacks FULL. Think of a class photo where absent students still get an empty chair with their name on it.",
 "more": "Real questions cross several tables, and each extra JOIN adds one table and one ON rule. To see which product each customer bought, you walk from customers to orders, then to order items, then to products. A self join joins a table to itself, which sounds odd but is very useful. Every employee has a manager id pointing at another employee, so two aliases of the same table show each person next to their boss. A cross join pairs every row with every row, which is handy for making every size and colour of a T shirt. But a forgotten ON rule can create a cross join by mistake, and the result explodes. Think of a family tree, where each person points at a parent in the same tree.",
 "onwhere": "Here is a subtle rule that catches many people. With an inner join, a filter can sit in ON or in WHERE with the same result. With an outer join, the place matters. A test in ON is part of the matching, so unmatched rows still survive with NULLs. A test in WHERE runs after the join, and since NULL never equals delivered, the padded rows are thrown away and your left join quietly becomes an inner join. Compare the two queries in the block. USING is a tidy shortcut when the join columns share a name. NATURAL JOIN guesses the columns for you, which is risky when tables change later. Think of choosing team members first by sport, then removing everyone without a jersey.",
 "levels": "One more climb. For a child, a join matches cards from two piles. At college level, a join is every possible pair filtered by the ON rule, and outer joins add back the lonely rows with NULLs. At masters level, engines use three main methods. A nested loop looks up partners for each row, a hash join builds a lookup table from the smaller side, and a merge join walks two sorted lists together. At research level, choosing the order of many joins is a hard puzzle, and PostgreSQL switches to a genetic search when a query joins twelve or more tables. Think of a wedding planner deciding which families to seat first."
});
